When merging two React projects, CSS conflicts can occur due to overlapping or contradictory styles. To resolve these issues, you can adopt multiple strategies, such as **CSS modules**, **namespacing**, and **scoped styles**. Below are suggestions and strategies to help isolate and prevent CSS from conflicting:

### 1. **Use CSS Modules**
   - CSS modules ensure that styles are scoped locally to the component, preventing conflicts with other styles.
   - Rename the `.css` files to `.module.css`.
   - Update your CSS imports and apply classes using `className={styles.someClass}`.

```css
/* index.module.css */
body {
  font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#c8d8d7, #979e9d);
}

#root {
  max-width: 50rem;
  margin: 2rem auto;
  padding: 2rem;
  background: linear-gradient(#35535b, #294a52);
  border-radius: 8px;
  color: white;
}

/* ... other styles */
```

```css
/* course.module.css */
body {
  font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#f0f6f8, #e0e1e7);
}

main {
  width: 90%;
  background-color: #d58d1f;
  padding: 2rem;
  color: #f0f8f1;
  border-radius: 6px;
}

/* ... other styles */
```

**Usage in components:**
```jsx
import styles from './index.module.css';

const IndexComponent = () => {
  return (
    <div className={styles.root}>
      {/* JSX content */}
    </div>
  );
};
```

### 2. **Scoped CSS (or BEM Naming Convention)**
   - Use more specific class names or scope the styles to a certain class to avoid conflicts.
   - You can use a BEM (Block, Element, Modifier) naming convention to avoid clashes.

```css
/* Scoped or BEM styles in index.css */
.index-body {
  background: linear-gradient(#c8d8d7, #979e9d);
  font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;
}

.index-root {
  background: linear-gradient(#35535b, #294a52);
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 2rem;
}
```

```css
/* Scoped styles in course.css */
.course-body {
  background: linear-gradient(#f0f6f8, #e0e1e7);
  font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
}

.course-main {
  background-color: #d58d1f;
  padding: 2rem;
  color: #f0f8f1;
  border-radius: 6px;
}
```

In your React component:
```jsx
<div className="index-root">
  {/* Index specific content */}
</div>
```

### 3. **Prefixing Classes**
   - If you can't use CSS Modules, manually prefix class names for better isolation.
   - This will avoid conflicts by giving unique class names to each project’s styles.

```css
/* index.css */
.index-body {
  font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#c8d8d7, #979e9d);
}

/* course.css */
.course-body {
  font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#f0f6f8, #e0e1e7);
}
```

### 4. **Use Styled-Components (CSS-in-JS)**
   - Using a CSS-in-JS solution like **styled-components** will scope the styles directly to the component, avoiding conflicts altogether.
   - This approach encapsulates your styles in JavaScript files, avoiding clashes with other components.

```jsx
import styled from 'styled-components';

const StyledRoot = styled.div`
  max-width: 50rem;
  margin: 2rem auto;
  padding: 2rem;
  background: linear-gradient(#35535b, #294a52);
  border-radius: 8px;
  color: white;
`;

const IndexComponent = () => (
  <StyledRoot>
    {/* Content */}
  </StyledRoot>
);
```

### 5. **Namespace the Project Styles**
   - Wrap each project’s CSS in a namespace by wrapping elements in a container with a unique class or ID.
   - Add a class to a container (`<div class="index-app">` for project A, `<div class="course-app">` for project B) and scope all CSS rules within that container.

```css
/* index.css */
.index-app body {
  font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#c8d8d7, #979e9d);
}

.index-app #root {
  background: linear-gradient(#35535b, #294a52);
  border-radius: 8px;
  padding: 2rem;
}
```

```css
/* course.css */
.course-app body {
  font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif;
  background: linear-gradient(#f0f6f8, #e0e1e7);
}

.course-app main {
  background-color: #d58d1f;
  padding: 2rem;
  color: #f0f8f1;
}
```

### 6. **Global vs Local Styles**
   - If some styles are supposed to be **global**, define them in a single shared file (like `global.css`), and keep the other styles scoped.

```css
/* global.css */
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* Specific styles are separated into their own CSS */
```

### 7. **Remove or Refactor Conflicting Global Styles**
   - Identify the most problematic styles and refactor them into more specific rules or namespaces.
   - Be cautious of universal selectors like `*`, `body`, or `ul` in global styles, as they can easily lead to conflicts across different components.

### Conclusion
You can fix the CSS conflicts in your combined React project by using one or more of these methods:
- **CSS Modules** for isolated styles.
- **Scoped or Namespaced CSS** using classes or IDs.
- **CSS-in-JS** solutions like styled-components.

If you want to match **multiple occurrences** of the `<span>` tag with `data-purpose="item-title"`, you can modify the regex to use the `g` (global) flag and iterate over all matches. Here's how you can extract all occurrences:

```javascript
// Example HTML string with multiple span elements
const htmlString = `
  <span data-purpose="item-title">Title 1</span>
  <span data-purpose="item-title">Title 2</span>
  <span data-purpose="item-title">Title 3</span>
`;

// Regular expression to match all occurrences
const regex = /<span data-purpose="item-title">(.*?)<\/span>/g;

let match;
const results = [];

// Iterate over all matches
while ((match = regex.exec(htmlString)) !== null) {
    results.push(match[1]);  // Add matched content to results array
}

// Log all matched content
console.log(results);  // Output: ["Title 1", "Title 2", "Title 3"]
```

Here, the `while` loop continues to execute as long as the regex finds matches, and it extracts all the contents inside the span elements. The `g` flag ensures that the regex searches for multiple matches rather than stopping after the first match.

This approach will collect all the text content between the `span` tags and store them in the `results` array.

