Yes, you can use regular expressions to extract the text from an HTML string, but it is not recommended to use regex for parsing HTML because it can lead to unpredictable results, especially with complex HTML structures. However, if you're working with a simple string and want to extract the content, you can use regex like this:

```javascript
// Example HTML string
const htmlString = '<span data-purpose="item-title">This is the title</span>';

// Regular expression to match the content inside the specific span tag
const regex = /<span data-purpose="item-title">(.*?)<\/span>/;

// Extract the content using the regular expression
const match = htmlString.match(regex);

if (match && match[1]) {
    console.log(match[1]);  // Output: "This is the title"
} else {
    console.log("No match found");
}
```

This regex looks for the `<span>` tag with `data-purpose="item-title"` and captures the text between the opening and closing tags.

However, **using the DOM API like `querySelector` is a more reliable approach for working with real HTML documents**. Regex should only be used for simple cases like this where you're working with an HTML string.

Here’s a JavaScript code snippet to get the text inside the `<span>` with `data-purpose="item-title"`:

```javascript
// Select the span element
const spanElement = document.querySelector('span[data-purpose="item-title"]');

// Get the text content inside the span element
const textContent = spanElement.textContent;

// Log the text content
console.log(textContent);
```

This code selects the `<span>` element with the `data-purpose="item-title"` attribute and retrieves its inner text using `textContent`.

There are five main ways to perform autowiring in Spring:

1. **byType**: Autowires by matching the property type with the bean type.
2. **byName**: Autowires by matching the bean name with the property name.
3. **constructor**: Autowires by constructor, matching the constructor parameter types with the bean types.
4. **@Autowired**: Uses annotations to mark fields, setters, or constructors for autowiring.
5. **@Qualifier**: Used alongside `@Autowired` when multiple beans of the same type exist, to specify the exact bean to inject by name. 

### Example for each:

#### 1. **byType**
```xml
<bean id="example" class="com.example.ClassExample" autowire="byType"/>
```

#### 2. **byName**
```xml
<bean id="example" class="com.example.ClassExample" autowire="byName"/>
```

#### 3. **constructor**
```xml
<bean id="example" class="com.example.ClassExample" autowire="constructor"/>
```

#### 4. **@Autowired**
```java
@Component
public class AutowireExample {
    @Autowired
    private Service service;
}
```

#### 5. **@Qualifier**
```java
@Component
public class AutowireExample {
    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("specificService")
    private Service service;
}
```

