Here's an enhanced version of the `fetchGeneric` function with options:

### Enhanced `fetchGeneric` with Options:
```ts
// Define FetchOptions type extending RequestInit for stricter control
interface FetchOptions extends RequestInit {
  method?: 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE' | 'PATCH'; // Strict typing for HTTP methods
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  body?: unknown; // Allow any type for the body, will be stringified
}

async function fetchGeneric<T>(url: string, options?: FetchOptions): Promise<T> {
  const { body, ...restOptions } = options || {};

  const response = await fetch(url, {
    ...restOptions,
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json', // Default to JSON headers
      ...options?.headers, // Merge with user-specified headers
    },
    body: body ? JSON.stringify(body) : undefined, // Stringify body if provided
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data: T = await response.json(); // Assuming JSON response
  return data;
}
```

### Usage Example:
```ts
interface ApiResponse {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

const getData = async () => {
  const data = await fetchGeneric<ApiResponse>('/api/data', {
    method: 'GET',
  });

  console.log(data.id, data.name);
};

const postData = async () => {
  const data = await fetchGeneric<ApiResponse>('/api/data', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: { name: 'New Item' }, // Body will be stringified to JSON
  });

  console.log(data.id, data.name);
};
```

### Support from Built-in Library for HTTP Methods:
While there’s no dedicated built-in library just for HTTP method names, TypeScript offers a way to strictly type them with string literal types as shown in the example (`'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE' | 'PATCH'`).

For more complex use cases, you can extend the `FetchOptions` or `RequestInit` with stricter typing for headers and methods. You can also refer to `RequestInit` in TypeScript, which is the built-in type that TypeScript provides for `fetch` options:

```ts
// Example: RequestInit from TypeScript
const options: RequestInit = {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ data: 'test' }),
};

fetch('/api/data', options);
```

In short, `fetchGeneric` can be extended for flexible API calls, while TypeScript already provides basic support via `RequestInit` for standard HTTP method names and request options.

