You're right — in the current code, the controller generates all 20 users first and then sends them with a delay of 100 ms per user. To stream each user every **1 ms** and send the data chunk by chunk, you'll need to adjust the `Thread.sleep` duration and ensure that the logic handles the streaming correctly.

Here’s the updated version:

```java
package com.p.java.playground.api.streamingResponse;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.mvc.method.annotation.ResponseBodyEmitter;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;

    @GetMapping(value = "/users/stream/v1", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    public ResponseBodyEmitter streamDummyUsersV1() {
        ResponseBodyEmitter emitter = new ResponseBodyEmitter();

        new Thread(() -> {
            try {
                // Generate 20 dummy users
                List<User> users = userService.generateDummyUsers(20);

                // Stream each user one by one
                for (User user : users) {
                    emitter.send(user); // Send each user as it's generated
                    Thread.sleep(1);    // Send each chunk with a delay of 1 ms
                }
                emitter.complete(); // Mark the streaming as complete
            } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
                emitter.completeWithError(e); // Handle any exceptions
            }
        }).start();

        return emitter;
    }
}
```

### Changes:
- The `Thread.sleep(100)` is replaced by `Thread.sleep(1)` to stream each user every **1 millisecond**.
- Users are still generated beforehand, but each one is sent to the client with minimal delay, chunk by chunk.

This will ensure that each user is streamed individually with a 1 ms delay between chunks, and the client can start processing users almost immediately as they are received.

The error message you're seeing, `jakarta.validation.NoProviderFoundException`, indicates that your application is trying to use the **Jakarta Bean Validation** API, but it can't find an implementation of it, such as **Hibernate Validator**.

### Solution

You need to add **Hibernate Validator** (which is the reference implementation for Jakarta Bean Validation) to your `pom.xml`.

### Step 1: Add Hibernate Validator Dependency

If you're using **Maven**, add the following dependency to your `pom.xml` file:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.hibernate.validator</groupId>
    <artifactId>hibernate-validator</artifactId>
    <version>8.0.0.Final</version> <!-- Ensure you use the latest stable version -->
</dependency>

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.glassfish</groupId>
    <artifactId>jakarta.el</artifactId>
    <version>4.0.2</version> <!-- Jakarta EL for Hibernate Validator -->
</dependency>
```

### Step 2: Rebuild and Restart the Application

After adding the dependencies, rebuild the project and restart your Spring Boot application. This should resolve the issue, as Hibernate Validator will now be available on the classpath and will provide the necessary Bean Validation functionality.

### Step 3: Verify the `MyService` Class
Once Hibernate Validator is included, the initialization of the `ValidatorFactory` in your `MyService` class should work without issues:

```java
import javax.validation.Validator;
import javax.validation.Validation;
import javax.validation.ValidatorFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
