#### b. Spring Boot Controller

```java
import org.apache.poi.xwpf.usermodel.XWPFDocument;
import org.apache.poi.xwpf.usermodel.XWPFParagraph;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.io.FileOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class DocxController {

    @PostMapping("/generate-docx")
    public ResponseEntity<?> generateDocx(@RequestBody Map<String, String> request) {
        String htmlContent = request.get("htmlContent");
        String outputFilePath = "server_folder/output.docx";  // Change to your server folder path

        try (XWPFDocument document = new XWPFDocument()) {
            // Add a paragraph to the document
            XWPFParagraph paragraph = document.createParagraph();
            paragraph.createRun().setText(htmlContent);  // You may need to format the content

            // Save the document to a folder
            Path outputPath = Paths.get(outputFilePath);
            try (FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream(outputPath.toFile())) {
                document.write(out);
            }
            return ResponseEntity.ok(Map.of("filePath", outputFilePath));
        } catch (IOException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(500).body("Error generating DOCX file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. Configuration

Make sure the folder where the DOCX will be saved has the proper write permissions for the Spring Boot application.

### How it works:
- The React frontend sends the HTML content to the Spring Boot backend.
- The backend generates a `.docx` file using Apache POI and saves it in the specified folder.
- The file path is returned to the frontend in the response.

