You can use JavaScript to dynamically add a `<style>` element to the `<head>` section of your HTML document. Here's how you can do it:

### Example: Add a `<style>` Element Using JavaScript
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Dynamic Style Injection</title>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="header">This is the header</div>
  <div class="content">This is the content</div>

  <script>
    // Create a <style> element
    const style = document.createElement('style');
    style.type = 'text/css';

    // Define CSS rules
    const css = `
      .header {
        color: white;
        background-color: #007bff;
        padding: 10px;
        text-align: center;
        font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      }
      .content {
        padding: 20px;
        font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      }
    `;

    // Add CSS rules to the <style> element
    style.appendChild(document.createTextNode(css));

    // Append the <style> element to the <head> section
    document.head.appendChild(style);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation
1. **Create the `<style>` Element**:  
   Use `document.createElement('style')` to create a `<style>` element.

2. **Add CSS Rules**:  
   Add CSS rules using `style.appendChild(document.createTextNode(css))`. Here, `css` is a string containing the CSS styles.

3. **Insert the `<style>` Element**:  
   Append the `<style>` element to the `<head>` section using `document.head.appendChild(style)`.

### Output
This dynamically adds the CSS styles defined in the JavaScript code to the `<head>` section of the document, styling the elements on the page.

### **🔐 Protect Routes with Authentication (JWT Middleware)**  

Now, let's add **middleware** to protect our routes. If a user is not authenticated (i.e., no valid JWT), they should be denied access.

---

### **1️⃣ Create `authMiddleware.ts`**
This middleware will verify JWT tokens before allowing access to protected routes.

```ts
import jwt from "jsonwebtoken";
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from "express";

const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET || "your_secret_key"; // Ensure you use a strong secret

export interface AuthRequest extends Request {
  user?: any; // Attach user data to the request object
}

export const authenticateUser = (req: AuthRequest, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;

  if (!authHeader || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
    return res.status(401).json({ message: "Unauthorized: No token provided" });
  }

  const token = authHeader.split(" ")[1];

  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = decoded; // Attach user info (id, email, role) to request
    next(); // Proceed to the protected route
  } catch (error) {
    return res.status(403).json({ message: "Forbidden: Invalid or expired token" });
  }
};
```

---

### **2️⃣ Apply Middleware to Protected Routes**
Modify your routes to use `authenticateUser`.

#### ✅ **Example: Protecting Profile Route**
```ts
import express from "express";
import { authenticateUser } from "../middlewares/authMiddleware";

const router = express.Router();

router.get("/profile", authenticateUser, (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    message: "Welcome to your profile!",
    user: req.user, // User data extracted from token
  });
});

export default router;
```

---

### **3️⃣ Testing the Protected Route**
1. **Try accessing `/profile` without a token** → You should get `{ message: "Unauthorized: No token provided" }`
2. **Send a valid JWT in the `Authorization` header** → You should get `{ message: "Welcome to your profile!" }` along with user details.

---

