import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.mapping.Document;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
@NoArgsConstructor
@Builder
public class ProblemPossibleSolution {
    @Id // Auto-generate ObjectId for the embedded document
    private String id;
    private String solutionDetail;
}
```

**Key Points:**
1. The `@Id` annotation tells Spring Data MongoDB to treat this field as a unique identifier.
2. The `id` field will be automatically populated when the parent document is saved, provided it's left as `null` during the upsert operation.

---

### Adjusting the `updateDescription` Method

If you're adding descriptions or solutions with autogenerated IDs, ensure you generate a new `ObjectId` when adding new descriptions. Here’s the modified method:

```java
import org.bson.types.ObjectId;

public Optional<Problem> updateDescription(String problemId, ProblemDescription updatedDescription) {
    Optional<Problem> problem = problemRepository.findById(problemId);

    problem.ifPresent(p -> {
        List<ProblemDescription> descriptions = p.getDescriptions();

        boolean exists = descriptions.stream()
            .anyMatch(desc -> desc.getId().equals(updatedDescription.getId()));

        if (exists) {
            // Update existing description
            descriptions.stream()
                .filter(desc -> desc.getId().equals(updatedDescription.getId()))
                .findFirst()
                .ifPresent(desc -> {
                    desc.setDetail(updatedDescription.getDetail());
                    desc.setLanguage(updatedDescription.getLanguage());
                });
        } else {
            // Add new description with auto-generated ID if not exists
            if (updatedDescription.getId() == null) {
                updatedDescription.setId(new ObjectId().toString());
            }
            descriptions.add(updatedDescription);
        }

        // Save the updated problem entity
        problemRepository.save(p);
    });

    return problem;
}
```

**Key Changes:**
1. **Generate ObjectId for New Descriptions:**
   - Use `new ObjectId().toString()` to generate a MongoDB-compatible unique ID.
   - Set this ID only if `updatedDescription.getId()` is `null`.

2. **Preserve Existing IDs:**
   - Existing `ProblemDescription` objects retain their IDs during updates.

---

### Example for New Descriptions

```java
ProblemDescription newDescription = ProblemDescription.builder()
    .detail("New detail")
    .language("EN")
    .build(); // No ID is set

problemService.updateDescription("problemId123", newDescription);
```

---

### Result

1. If the description already exists, it will be updated.
2. If the description is new (i.e., `id` is null or missing), a unique `ObjectId` will be assigned.
3. When the parent `Problem` is saved, the embedded documents will contain autogenerated IDs for new entries.

