To extract the common logic for modals, we can create a reusable modal component. This will handle the logic for form submissions and closing actions, which can be used in your `UpsertSolutionModal`, `UpsertProblemModal`, and `UpsertDescriptionModal` components. We'll use a generic modal component that can accept different inputs and logic depending on the form being used.

### 1. Create the `ModalForm.tsx` (Common Form Modal Component)

```tsx
import React, { useState } from "react";

interface ModalFormProps {
  title: string;
  fields: { label: string; value: string; setValue: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<string>> }[];
  onSubmit: () => void;
  onClose: () => void;
  submitButtonText: string;
}

const ModalForm: React.FC<ModalFormProps> = ({
  title,
  fields,
  onSubmit,
  onClose,
  submitButtonText,
}) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <form>
        {fields.map((field, index) => (
          <div key={index}>
            <label>
              {field.label}:
              <input value={field.value} onChange={(e) => field.setValue(e.target.value)} />
            </label>
          </div>
        ))}
        <button type="button" onClick={onSubmit}>
          {submitButtonText}
        </button>
        <button type="button" onClick={onClose}>
          Cancel
        </button>
      </form>
    </div>
  );
};

export default ModalForm;
```

### Explanation of `ModalForm`:
- **Fields**: The `fields` prop takes an array of objects, where each object contains a label, value, and a setter function to update the input value.
- **Submit and Close**: The `onSubmit` function is triggered when the form is submitted, and `onClose` is used to close the modal.
- **Dynamic**: This modal can be used for any form that accepts a set of fields, with dynamic titles and submit button text.

