Now, we will update the `ProblemController` to throw the custom exceptions where appropriate.

#### Updated `ProblemController.java`

```java
package com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.controller;

import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.exception.ProblemNotFoundException;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.exception.DescriptionNotFoundException;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.exception.SolutionNotFoundException;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.model.Problem;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.model.ProblemDescription;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.model.ProblemPossibleSolution;
import com.p.problem.mgmt.nosql.service.ProblemService;
import java.io.Serializable;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/problems")
public class ProblemController {

  @Autowired private ProblemService problemService;

  // Get all problems
  @GetMapping
  public List<Map<String, ? extends Serializable>> getAllProblems() {
    return problemService.getAllProblems().stream()
        .map(
            problem ->
                Map.of(
                    "id",
                    problem.getId(),
                    "title",
                    problem.getTitle(),
                    "status",
                    problem.getStatus()))
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
  }

  // Get problem details by ID
  @GetMapping("/{id}")
  public ResponseEntity<Problem> getProblemById(@PathVariable("id") String id) {
    Optional<Problem> problem = problemService.getProblemById(id);
    if (problem.isPresent()) {
      return ResponseEntity.ok(problem.get());
    } else {
      throw new ProblemNotFoundException("Problem with ID " + id + " not found");
    }
  }

  // Upsert a problem
  @PostMapping
  public ResponseEntity<Problem> upsertProblem(@RequestBody Problem problem) {
    Problem savedProblem = problemService.upsertProblem(problem);
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(savedProblem);
  }

  // Upsert a problem description
  @PostMapping("/{problemId}/descriptions")
  public ResponseEntity<Problem> upsertDescription(
      @PathVariable("problemId") String problemId, @RequestBody ProblemDescription description) {
    Optional<Problem> updatedProblem =
        problemService.upsertDescriptionToProblem(problemId, description);
    if (updatedProblem.isPresent()) {
      return ResponseEntity.ok(updatedProblem.get());
    } else {
      throw new DescriptionNotFoundException("Description for Problem ID " + problemId + " not found");
    }
  }

  // Upsert a problem solution
  @PostMapping("/{problemId}/solutions")
  public ResponseEntity<Problem> upsertSolution(
      @PathVariable("problemId") String problemId, @RequestBody ProblemPossibleSolution solution) {
    Optional<Problem> updatedProblem = problemService.addSolutionToProblem(problemId, solution);
    if (updatedProblem.isPresent()) {
      return ResponseEntity.ok(updatedProblem.get());
    } else {
      throw new SolutionNotFoundException("Solution for Problem ID " + problemId + " not found");
    }
  }
}
```

### Explanation of Changes:
1. **Custom Exception Classes**: 
   - `ProblemNotFoundException`, `DescriptionNotFoundException`, and `SolutionNotFoundException` have been created to handle specific exceptions.

2. **ControlAdvice**:
   - The `ControlAdvice` class is annotated with `@RestControllerAdvice` to handle global exceptions. It has methods for handling the custom exceptions and returning appropriate HTTP responses with a message.

3. **ProblemController**:
   - The `ProblemController` has been updated to throw the custom exceptions (`ProblemNotFoundException`, `DescriptionNotFoundException`, and `SolutionNotFoundException`) when a resource is not found, instead of returning `ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND).body(null)`.

This approach ensures that exceptions are consistently handled and the client receives meaningful error messages with appropriate HTTP status codes.

