The issue lies in the placement of the generic declaration `<T>` in the statement. In Java, you can't declare a generic type in the middle of a variable declaration. Instead, the generic type needs to be declared at the class level or method level, not directly for the field.

Here’s the corrected code:

### Corrected Code

```java
private static final BiFunction<Optional<Object>, String, ResponseEntity<Object>> HANDLE_OPTIONAL = 
    (optionalData, errorMessage) -> {
        if (optionalData.isPresent()) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(optionalData.get());
        } else {
            throw new BusinessDataNotFoundException(errorMessage);
        }
    };
```

### For Generic Support

To allow support for generic `<T>`, you need to define a method instead of using a `static final` field, as generics can't be directly applied to fields.

```java
public static <T> BiFunction<Optional<T>, String, ResponseEntity<T>> handleOptional() {
    return (optionalData, errorMessage) -> {
        if (optionalData.isPresent()) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(optionalData.get());
        } else {
            throw new BusinessDataNotFoundException(errorMessage);
        }
    };
}
```

### Usage Example

```java
Optional<String> optionalValue = Optional.of("Hello World");
ResponseEntity<String> response = handleOptional().apply(optionalValue, "Data not found");
System.out.println(response.getBody()); // Prints: Hello World
```

In this approach, the `handleOptional` method allows the lambda to remain generic.

