Yes, in a Spring Boot project, you can use `ResponseEntity` to prepare both a custom message and body. Here's an example:

### Example with Custom Message and Body

```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
public class MyController {

    @GetMapping("/example")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> getExample() {
        // Prepare a custom message and body
        Map<String, Object> responseBody = new HashMap<>();
        responseBody.put("message", "Request processed successfully");
        responseBody.put("data", "This is your response body content");

        // Return ResponseEntity with status
        return new ResponseEntity<>(responseBody, HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

### Explanation of the Code:
1. **Custom Message:** The `"message"` field in the response body can carry a message such as "Request processed successfully."
2. **Custom Body:** The `"data"` field can carry the main content or payload of the response.
3. **ResponseEntity:** Combines the body and the HTTP status code (`HttpStatus.OK`).

### JSON Output
When you hit the endpoint `/example`, the response might look like this:

```json
{
  "message": "Request processed successfully",
  "data": "This is your response body content"
}
```

This approach allows you to send structured responses with a message, data, and HTTP status code.

Here’s a revised version of the `TopicSectionCard` component with optimizations such as `React.memo` for performance, restructuring inline styles to use `useMemo` for style memoization, and minor improvements for readability and maintainability:

```javascript
import React, { memo, useMemo } from "react";
import CustomButton from "../../../../common/components/custom-button/CustomButton";
import HoverableSpan from "../../../../common/components/hoverable-span/HoverableSpan";
import { SmartPreviewer } from "../../../../common/components/smart-editor/SmartEditorV3";
import ToggleablePanel from "../../../../common/components/toggleable-panel/ToggleablePanel";
import ListSection from "../../../../common/components/list-section/ListSection";
import { topicAndSectionStyles as styles } from "../styles";

const TopicSectionCard = ({
  data: ts,
  tags = [],
  selectedElementRef,
  selectedSectionId,
  onEditSection = () => {},
  onLinkedTagSelection = () => {},
}) => {
  const handleLinkedTagSelection = (linkedTagUID) => {
    onLinkedTagSelection(linkedTagUID);
  };

  const filteredTags = useMemo(
    () =>
      ts?.tags?.map((uniqueId) =>
        tags.find((tag) => tag.uniqueId === uniqueId)
      ) || [],
    [ts?.tags, tags]
  );

  const containerStyle = useMemo(
    () => ({
      border: "1px solid #999", // Grey border
      padding: "2px 5px", // Adjust padding as needed
      borderRadius: "4px",
      marginBottom: "10px",
    }),
    []
  );

  const tagPanelStyle = useMemo(
    () => ({
      backgroundColor: "lemonchiffon",
      border: "1px solid #999", // Grey border
      padding: "2px 5px", // Adjust padding as needed
      borderRadius: "4px",
      marginBottom: "10px",
    }),
    []
  );

  if (!ts || !ts.name) {
    return <>Section data is null</>;
  }

  return (
    <ToggleablePanel title={ts.name} showContent={true}>
      <div key={ts.uniqueId} style={containerStyle}>
        <span
          ref={selectedSectionId === ts.uniqueId ? selectedElementRef : null}
        ></span>

        <div style={{ margin: "10px 0" }}>
          <ListSection
            title=""
            errorMessage=""
            items={[
              {
                id: 1,
                title: "Edit",
                action: () => onEditSection(ts.uniqueId),
              },
              {
                id: 2,
                title: "Move up",
                action: () => console.log("To be implemented!!"),
              },
              {
                id: 3,
                title: "Move down",
                action: () => console.log("To be implemented!!"),
              },
            ]}
            renderItem={({ id, title, action }) => (
              <CustomButton
                key={id}
                style={{ ...styles.topicTagStyle, marginRight: "10px" }}
                onClick={action}
              >
                {title}
              </CustomButton>
            )}
          />
        </div>

        <SmartPreviewer data={ts.smartContent} />

        {filteredTags.length > 0 && (
          <ToggleablePanel
            panelContainerStyle={tagPanelStyle}
            title="Tags:-"
            showContent={true}
          >
            <ListSection
              errorMessage=""
              items={filteredTags}
              title=""
              renderItem={(tag) =>
                tag && (
                  <HoverableSpan
                    style={{ ...styles.topicTagStyle, margin: "5px" }}
                    key={tag._id}
                    onClick={() => handleLinkedTagSelection(tag.uniqueId)}
                  >
                    {tag.title}
                  </HoverableSpan>
                )
              }
            />
          </ToggleablePanel>
        )}
      </div>
    </ToggleablePanel>
  );
};

export default memo(TopicSectionCard);
```

### Optimizations:
1. **React.memo**:
   - The `TopicSectionCard` is memoized with `React.memo` to prevent unnecessary re-renders if props haven't changed.
2. **useMemo for styles**:
   - Inline styles (`containerStyle` and `tagPanelStyle`) are memoized using `useMemo` to avoid unnecessary recalculations on each render.
3. **useMemo for filteredTags**:
   - `filteredTags` is memoized to avoid recalculating the filtered tags array when `tags` or `ts?.tags` haven't changed.
4. **Removed redundant checks**:
   - Removed unnecessary `filteredTags &&` in conditions as the default value already handles null or undefined cases.

This ensures improved performance, particularly in scenarios where the component is rendered frequently or with a large dataset.

